config {
  type: "view",
  schema: constants.SCHEMAS.cm360_staging,
  name: "stg_cm360_reach",
  tags: ["cm360", "staging"],
  description: "CM360 reach and the authoritative click count, at campaign x day. Reads cm360_reachv2 since 2026-11-11, de-duplicated largest-wins since 2026-11-16.",
  columns: {
    total_reach: "NON-ADDITIVE. De-duplicated by CM360 per row. Monthly reach is not the sum of daily reach.",
    is_reach_suppressed: "TRUE where CM360 returned something non-numeric — a privacy suppression, not a zero. 620 of 3,981 rows on 2026-11-11, 15.6%.",
    clicks: "THE authoritative CM360 click count, and what v_campaign_daily publishes. The detail reports lose clicks to their own dimension count; this one does not."
  }
}

-- THE DE-DUPLICATION IS BACK, 2026-11-16, AND TAKING IT OUT WAS A
-- MISTAKE. This file explains both halves honestly because the second
-- half is the useful part.
--
-- WHY IT CAME OUT ON 11-11. cm360_reachv2 arrived holding 3,981 rows for
-- 3,981 keys across all 60 days, the first CM360 source with no
-- duplication at all. The old cm360_reach appended its window instead of
-- replacing it -- 12,547 rows for 2,756 keys, 4.55 copies each -- and the
-- largest-wins QUALIFY written to survive that was not free: taking the
-- maximum impressions across up to seven reads overstates them, because
-- the maximum is whichever snapshot ran highest, not whichever ran last.
-- Reconciled over the 43 overlapping days it ran 0.03% high on
-- impressions and 0.004% on clicks -- small, but a bias rather than
-- noise. On a source with one row per key there was nothing to choose
-- between and nothing to bias, so it went.
--
-- THE REASONING FOR LEAVING IT OUT WAS WRONG, and this is the part worth
-- remembering. The file said: fct_cm360_reach_daily already asserts
-- uniqueKey on date + advertiser_id + campaign_id, so the day the source
-- changes the workflow turns red instead. It cannot. The mart is
--
--   SELECT date, advertiser_id, campaign_id, SUM(impressions), ...
--   GROUP BY 1, 2, 3
--
-- Two source rows on one key are ADDED into one mart row. The key is
-- unique by construction, the assertion passes on every run, and the
-- metrics are silently doubled. A uniqueKey assertion on a mart that
-- aggregates tests the GROUP BY, not the data.
--
-- WHAT HAPPENED ON 11-16. CM360 resumed after five days frozen and
-- re-appended its window. 4,429 rows for 4,390 keys: 39 duplicated keys,
-- all pairs, all on 11-07 to 11-10, each pair the same campaign-day read
-- twice about 0.2% apart. Summed by the mart:
--
--   zi            clickuri raport   corect
--   2026-11-07    20,310            19,186     +5.9%
--   2026-11-08    21,406            19,592     +9.3%
--   2026-11-09    31,338            20,997    +49.2%
--   2026-11-10    35,035            27,115    +29.2%
--
-- Published through v_campaign_daily as THE CM360 click count, with
-- every assertion green.
--
-- SO: QUALIFY HERE, AND A SEPARATE ASSERTION THAT READS THE SOURCE.
-- The QUALIFY makes the numbers right today. assert_cm360_reach_grain
-- reads cm360_reachv2 directly, where the mart's GROUP BY cannot hide
-- anything, so the duplication stays VISIBLE instead of being silently
-- absorbed. Neither alone is enough -- that was the whole lesson.
--
-- ORDERED BY IMPRESSIONS, as it was before, because impressions climb
-- across snapshots while reach wobbles as CM360 refines it. The known
-- cost is the one measured above: a fraction of a percent high. That is
-- the right trade against tens of percent wrong.
--
-- CROSS-VALIDATED AGAINST A DIFFERENT EXTRACTION, 2026-11-11. Over the
-- full 60 days cm360_basic, which is campaign x site x creative and comes
-- through its own report, totalled 1,130,840 clicks. So did this. To the
-- unit. Impressions landed 125 apart in 284 million. That test is worth
-- repeating once both sources are clean again -- cm360_basic started
-- duplicating on the same morning and for the same reason.
--
-- REACH CAN EXCEED IMPRESSIONS ON 136 ROWS, and both cases are benign.
-- "DART Search" carries clicks and reach against zero impressions,
-- because CM360 only ever tracked the click -- the impression happened on
-- Google. The rest are BidManager campaigns where a modelled reach rounds
-- about 0.1% above a small impression count: 8,430 against 8,422.
--
-- STILL STRING DATE AND FLOAT CLICKS. parseCm360Date handles the first.
-- The second has no fractional values, checked, so CAST is safe. Neither
-- is worth a workaround here; both are on the list for Keboola.

SELECT
  CAST(profileId AS STRING)         AS profile_id,
  profileName                       AS profile_name,
  ${metrics.parseCm360Date("date")} AS date,
  CAST(advertiserId AS STRING)      AS advertiser_id,
  NULLIF(TRIM(advertiser), '')      AS advertiser_name,
  CAST(campaignId AS STRING)        AS campaign_id,
  NULLIF(TRIM(campaign), '')        AS campaign_name,

  -- strip thousands separators and spaces, then cast
  SAFE_CAST(REGEXP_REPLACE(uniqueReachTotalReach, r'[^0-9]', '') AS INT64) AS total_reach,
  uniqueReachTotalReach                                                    AS total_reach_raw,
  (uniqueReachTotalReach IS NOT NULL
   AND SAFE_CAST(REGEXP_REPLACE(uniqueReachTotalReach, r'[^0-9]', '') AS INT64) IS NULL)
                                                                           AS is_reach_suppressed,

  CAST(impressions AS INT64) AS impressions,
  CAST(clicks      AS INT64) AS clicks
FROM ${ref({ schema: constants.DATASETS.cm360, name: "cm360_reachv2" })}
QUALIFY ROW_NUMBER() OVER (
  PARTITION BY date, advertiserId, campaignId
  ORDER BY impressions DESC, clicks DESC
) = 1
